Start();
include(utilities);
search reset stack Grokking;
search GroupFields Utilities list;

/*****************************************************************************
    altShadowT -- WHY A MEMBER-SHAPED GRAMMAR RULE CANNOT CARRY A GENERATED
    BODY. Measured 2026-08-18, with a negative control.

    THE FINDING. In GroupItem.twk parse, the arm that runs testAttributes is
    tested BEFORE the arm that runs testOptions, and the two are chained with
    or. So hasAttributes SHADOWS hasMembers. A rule whose terms are members is
    an alternation and reaches testOptions only while it owns no attributes at
    all. Hang ONE attribute on it and the alternation is never consulted again.

    WHY THAT BITES THE PARSE WALK. genParseTest attaches its generated CodE
    with the add-attribute operator, and processCode attaches the resulting
    BlocK the same way. Both are noPrint. testAttributes skips every noPrint
    attribute and therefore returns false, so an alternation rule that has been
    handed a body stops matching anything at all, and the parse that was
    reading the body runs off the end of its input.

    THE PARTITION IT PRODUCES, measured on eleven rules with an EMPTY body, so
    the body text cannot be the variable. Every attribute-shaped rule compiled.
    Every member-shaped rule refused. QuotE, StringXP, TokenXP, Braced, BlocK,
    Iterate and Xpress are the ok side. ANYorNum, StatemenT, WardeD and
    InvokeArg are the refused side.

    ROW A IS THE NEGATIVE CONTROL AND IT IS THE ROW THAT MAKES THIS A FINDING
    RATHER THAN A CORRELATION. It hangs a single noPrint attribute on ANYorNum.
    No body, no compile, no isCoded, nothing to do with generation. If the
    shadowing is the mechanism, the very next INVOCATION in this file cannot be
    parsed. Expect RunRulE expected a method not, on stderr, naming row B.

    ROW B IS AN ACTION CALL WITH AN ARGUMENT, AND THE SHAPE IS LOAD-BEARING.
    A first draft used a cerr line and it PARSED FINE with the poison in place,
    which would have read as the defect being absent. ANYorNum sits under
    TokenXP, so it is reached when the parser resolves a name followed by an
    invocation argument. A bare keyword statement never asks for it. Match the
    probe to the path or the probe answers a different question.

    READING IT. Row B failing to parse IS the pass. If row B runs and prints,
    the shadowing has been fixed or the rule is no longer on the path that
    parses an invocation, and this fixture needs re-deriving before it is
    cited. The sentinel therefore must NOT appear while the defect stands.
*****************************************************************************/

register(Utilities);
define
    dummy;
    rowB argument code={
        cerr "B  FAIL  the invocation parsed -- alternation survived the poison":;
        };
    poison argument code={
        dud <- copyOf(dummy);
        dud :. noPrinT;
        argument +% dud;
        cerr "A  poisoned" taG "with one noPrint attribute -- row B must now fail to parse":;
        };
    ;
poison(ANYorNum);
rowB(dummy);
cerr "ALTSHADOWT SENTINEL -- reaching here means the defect is GONE":;
stop();
